1. This software's own code

MFF Timing Inspector was developed by P. Molfese, Center for Multimodal
Neuroimaging (CMN), National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH), National
Institutes of Health (NIH) (https://cmn.nimh.nih.gov), as part of official
duties for the United States Government.

This software is a "work of the United States Government" under 17 U.S.C. § 105
and is not subject to copyright protection within the United States.
International copyright protections may apply.

To the extent that any rights can be asserted by the United States Government,
the Government makes its own contributions to MFF Timing Inspector available
for public use, copying, modification, and redistribution without fee or
copyright restriction.

Nothing elsewhere in this notice narrows that grant. The Government-authored
files remain a public work in their own right: they may be extracted from
MFF Timing Inspector and reused under no conditions at all, whatever license
governs the assembled application.


2. Third-party components

As of this writing, MFF Timing Inspector does not incorporate, vendor, or
redistribute any third-party source code, libraries, models, or data. It is
built entirely on Apple's own system frameworks (SwiftUI, Swift Charts,
Foundation), which ship with Xcode and macOS and are licensed separately by
Apple, not by this repository.

If third-party code is ever added, that code's own license governs it, and the
United States Government cannot waive, grant, or modify rights held by third
parties.


3. Data files

Files under `example_files/` and `MFFTimingToolTests/Fixtures/` are sample or
synthetic timing data used to develop and test this software, not part of the
software itself. Some of that data was derived from a real recording session;
see the note in README.md before publishing this repository anywhere public.
